Durarara!! AU
Chapter 6 
Curtis finally reached the flat.
It was nicer than most people would expect from someone who spent his first night in the city sleeping in a net café and bleeding on sidewalks. Two bedrooms. Fully furnished. Rent paid in advance for the next ten years. He had arranged it before he ever set foot in Japan—one less thing to think about once he arrived.
He shut the door behind him, locked it, and leaned against it for a second longer than necessary. The stitches pulled. The head wound ached with a dull, steady pulse. He ignored both.
First order of business: money.
He pulled a heavy duffle bag from the back of the closet and set it on the bed. The zipper opened to neat, dense stacks of cash. Enough to buy a motorcycle outright and still have plenty left over. He took what he needed, closed the bag, and put it away again.
Next: clothes.
Curtis stood in front of the mirror and looked at the ruined white shirt, the dried blood, the general disaster of his current state. For a moment he considered the usual route—mask, sunglasses, hoodie, the full night version of himself. It would have been easier. Familiar.
He dismissed the idea almost immediately.
A masked man walking into a motorcycle dealership with a bag of cash was the fastest way to get the police called. They would never let him near the Harleys looking like that.
He needed to look like someone who belonged on the property.
Curtis opened the second closet.
Most of the clothes inside were practical. Dark. Easy to move in. But hanging near the back was the jacket Justice had left him—gray with deep red lining and accents, structured, a little sharp. The kind of thing his friend had always said suited him better than the hoodies and masks. Curtis had never worn it much. It felt like putting on a version of himself that belonged to someone else’s hopes.
He put it on anyway.
White shirt. Red tie. The jacket over top. Black pants. He left the long braids free and settled a red Jumpman cap over them, tugging the brim low enough to throw a little shadow without fully hiding his face. The mismatched eyes still showed. The scars on his scalp were mostly covered. The fresh stitches on his shoulder sat beneath the fabric, out of sight.
He checked the mirror once.
It wasn’t the masked version of him.
It wasn’t the school uniform version either.
It was something closer to the boy in the photograph—just older, harder, and still carrying the weight of everything that had come after.
Curtis grabbed the cash, pocketed his phone, and left the flat.
He had a motorcycle to buy.
Curtis locked the flat behind him and started walking, the structured gray-and-red jacket settling across his shoulders with an unfamiliar weight. The red Jumpman cap sat low over his braids. For the first time since arriving in Ikebukuro, he looked like someone who could walk into a dealership without immediately being asked to leave.
Halfway down the block he pulled his phone out again and reopened the chat room.
New messages had stacked up while he was changing.
TarouTanaka:
I really wasn’t going to join…
I was just curious about the rumors.
Kanra:
Curiosity is how people end up in interesting situations, TarouTanaka~
Some of those situations are harder to leave than others.
Setton:
Kanra. Stop.
Kanra:
I’m only offering friendly advice ♪
Hanma:
The Dollars are bigger than most people think.
Be careful who you listen to.
Setton:
…
TarouTanaka:
Who is Hanma?
No reply from Hanma after that.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes moved over the exchange once. Kanra’s tone still scraped against his nerves in a way he didn’t like. Setton’s warnings were consistent. The new username—Hanma—sat in the middle of it all with the same short, flat style as the earlier “Hello.”
He didn’t type anything.
He locked the phone, slid it back into the jacket pocket, and kept walking toward the motorcycle shop. The cash was secure. The stitches were holding. And for once he looked like a man who could put money down on a Harley without anyone asking the wrong kind of questions.
The chat could wait.
Curtis was halfway down the next block when his hand brushed an inner pocket of the jacket and found something unexpected.
He pulled it out.
A pair of gold-framed Louis Vuitton sunglasses. Still clean. Still sharp. Another thing Justice had left behind without explanation—tucked into the coat like it belonged there.
Curtis turned them over once in his fingers, then slid them on.
The world dimmed into a cooler tint. The mismatched eyes disappeared behind the reflective lenses. Combined with the red Jumpman cap and the structured gray-and-red jacket, the effect was immediate. He no longer looked like a bloodied transfer student trying to clean up. He looked deliberate. Expensive. A little dangerous.
A small, private smile tugged at the corner of his mouth.
It’s almost as if you left this behind so I can still hide my face.
Though you always liked being flashy.
The thought settled warm and aching behind the new lenses.
A few faces turned as he passed. Not the wary stares he got when he was bleeding and masked—something closer to curiosity, interest, the kind of double-take people gave when someone walked like they belonged exactly where they were. Curtis ignored them the same way he ignored most attention, hands in the jacket pockets, long black braids falling neatly down his chest as he continued toward the motorcycle shop.
For the first time since arriving in Ikebukuro, he looked like someone Justice would have been proud to walk beside.
He kept the smile a moment longer, then let it fade as the dealership came back into view.
Curtis slowed at the next crosswalk and pulled his phone out again.
The chat was still active. TarouTanaka was asking questions. Kanra was being Kanra. Setton remained cautious. His own previous messages sat there under the name Hanma—short, flat, offering nothing extra.
He considered leaving it that way.
Then he started typing.
Hanma:
Name’s Hanma.
I’m new to the city.
That’s all.
He sent it, locked the phone, and kept walking.
No long introduction. No details about where he came from or what he could do. Just enough to stop being a silent username that only appeared to drop warnings. The gold-framed sunglasses caught the late light as he moved, red cap low, Justice’s jacket sharp against the evening street.
In the chat, the message sat there waiting for whatever reaction it would get.
Curtis didn’t check for replies right away.
He had a motorcycle to buy first.
